WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.340
Most of Ras sits on the inner face of the plasma membrane.

2
00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:11.620
Yet Ras has no stretch of amino acids that crosses a membrane.

3
00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:13.460
So what holds it there?

4
00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:19.140
Ras relays a growth signal inward from a receptor, so where it sits matters.

5
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:21.980
The answer is a fatty chain.

6
00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:29.010
Cells attach fatty chains to proteins, using dedicated enzymes.

7
00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:32.970
On Ras, that chain is called farnesyl.

8
00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:39.150
A long chain is comfortable buried in a membrane, and uncomfortable in water.

9
00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:44.290
But one short chain is a weak grip, and chains pop out again.

10
00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:50.430
A protein held this way visits a membrane and leaves it, still tied to its chain.

11
00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:55.870
So cells add a second signal, of one of two kinds.

12
00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:02.830
One kind is a second fatty chain, as in H-Ras, which carries farnesyl plus

13
00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:07.210
palmitate. The other kind is a patch of positive charge.

14
00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:12.290
K-Ras4B carries farnesyl plus a run of lysines.

15
00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:16.790
Those lysines are attracted to the negative lipids of the inner face.

16
00:01:19.083 --> 00:01:24.183
Binding free energies add, which means affinities multiply.

17
00:01:25.283 --> 00:01:31.763
Each extra 1.4 kilocalories per mole at body temperature, makes binding about ten

18
00:01:31.763 --> 00:01:36.663
times tighter. So two weak grips make one strong grip.

19
00:01:37.603 --> 00:01:43.723
Think of hooks. One hook holds a coat until someone bumps it, and two hooks hold it.

20
00:01:44.517 --> 00:01:50.657
The second signal is also a handle for control: remove it and the protein drops off.

21
00:01:51.577 --> 00:01:57.397
In K-Ras4B, protein kinase C puts a phosphate next to the lysines.

22
00:01:58.117 --> 00:02:02.037
The phosphate adds negative charge beside a positive patch.

23
00:02:02.777 --> 00:02:06.997
The attraction to the membrane weakens, and K-Ras4B is released.

24
00:02:07.837 --> 00:02:10.817
This is called a farnesyl-electrostatic switch.

25
00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:18.710
H-Ras carries a second fatty chain, palmitate, joined by a bond that is easily cut.

26
00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:22.750
Enzymes called thioesterases cut that bond.

27
00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:27.590
So palmitate is the one chain cells can put on and take off.

28
00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:32.470
Once it is gone, the grip weakens, and H-Ras drifts away.

29
00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:39.870
So keep this rule: one chain visits, a second signal holds, and removing that signal

30
00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:41.270
releases the protein.
